{"name":"Queen of Hearts","hex":"#98333a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Queen of Hearts","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of queen of hearts."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Queen_of_Hearts"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Queen of Hearts (poem)","blocks":[{"text":"\"The Queen of Hearts\" is an English poem and nursery rhyme based on the characters found on playing cards, written by an anonymous author, originally published with three lesser-known stanzas, \"The King of Spades\", \"The King of Clubs\", and \"The Diamond King\", in the British publication The European Magazine, vol. 1, no. 4, in April 1782. However, Iona and Peter Opie have argued that there is evidence to suggest that these other stanzas were later additions to an older poem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Queen_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg/330px-Queen_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Queen_of_Hearts_(poem)"},{"title":"Wilkie Collins bibliography","blocks":[{"text":"This is a bibliography of the works of Wilkie Collins."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/98/Millais_Wilkie_Collins.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilkie_Collins_bibliography"},{"title":"Queen of Hearts (Alice's Adventures in Wonderland)","blocks":[{"text":"The Queen of Hearts is a fictional character and the main antagonist in the 1865 book Alice's Adventures in Wonderland by Lewis Carroll. She is a childish, foul-tempered monarch whom Carroll himself describes as \"a blind fury\", and who is quick to give death sentences at even the slightest of offenses. One of her most famous lines is the oft-repeated \"Off with his/her/their head(s)!\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/John_Tenniel_-_Illustration_from_The_Nursery_Alice_%281890%29_-_c06544_02.jpg/330px-John_Tenniel_-_Illustration_from_The_Nursery_Alice_%281890%29_-_c06544_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_of_Hearts_(Alice's_Adventures_in_Wonderland)"},{"title":"Queen (playing card)","blocks":[{"text":"The queen is a playing card with a picture of a queen on it. In many European languages, the king and queen begin with the same letter so the latter is often called dame (lady) or variations thereof. In French playing cards, the usual rank of a queen is between the king and the jack. In tarot decks, it outranks the knight which in turn outranks the jack. In the Spanish deck and some Italian decks, the Queen does not exist and the Horse or Bull appears in them instead, with the same role and value."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Queen_playing_cards.jpg/330px-Queen_playing_cards.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen_(playing_card)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg/500px-Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg/1280px-Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg/500px-Knave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Knave of Hearts. Illustration by W. W. Denslow.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKnave_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/King_of_Hearts_Mother_Goose2.jp